module gunbc.serving.serving_enrollment

import std.types { String, NonEmptyStr, List, Port }
import std.nat { Nat }
import v2.std.optional { Present, Absent }
import product.placement_supply { HostIdentity }
import gunbc.fleet_intent_network { host_lan_address }
import gunbc.spark.fabric_switch_observed { FabricGroup, FabricGroupA, FabricGroupB, fabric_group_wire }
import gunbc.spark.pair_serving_desired { spark_pair_serving_head }
import gunbc.spark.pair_serving_realization { spark_pair_serve_port }
import gunbc.spark.glm_canary_converge { glm_canary_arm, SeqBound, ExperimentalOperatorBound, MeasuredFromLaunchLine }

// ── WHO WE MAY ASK IS NOT WHOSE HOSTS WE CLAIM ────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE ROW THIS MODULE SPLITS, AND WHY SPLITTING IT IS THE WHOLE CHANGE. One list --
// gunbc.spark.pair_serving_desired spark_pair_serving_groups -- answered two questions that move for
// different reasons. It is the subject of spark_pair_serving_desired, so a group listed there is a
// group whose HOSTS THIS REPOSITORY CLAIMS AND CONVERGES; and it was also the roster the harness
// walked to decide WHICH ENDPOINTS A TURN MAY BE OFFERED. Those coincided only while one route
// existed.
//
// THEY STOPPED COINCIDING ON 2026-09-08, and the withdrawal that followed is the reason group B
// cannot simply be added back to that row. Group B's head is srv9, which is the host
// gunbc.spark.glm_canary_converge names: leaving B in the convergence roster left two authorities
// claiming one machine, and the first pair-serving convergence to run would have acted on a host it
// believed idle. The operator authorised removing the CLAIM ("you can stop group b or both, it's up
// to you"), and what was withdrawn was a claim on hosts -- explicitly not a front door. Re-adding B
// to that row would re-create the double claim this lane has no authority to restore.
//
// SO ENROLMENT IS ITS OWN AUTHORITY AND CARRIES BOTH GROUPS. A route here is a thing we may ASK; it
// is not a thing we deploy, converge, restart or size. Group B is enrolled while its hosts stay
// unclaimed, which is exactly the state the fleet is in: the canary is serving on srv9 and nothing
// in this repository launched it through the pair route.
//
// AND THE SAME SPLIT ANSWERS THE SECOND DEFECT. Membership in the convergence roster was being read
// as an answer to "who can serve now", which it never was: a declared head that is down stayed a
// candidate, and the probe that would have said so ran AFTER the roster had already chosen the
// population. Enrolment answers only "who we may ask"; every route below still has to answer a
// probe before any turn is offered to it, and gunbc.harness.harness_backend is where that happens.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES TO THE OPEN QUESTION OF WHERE SERVING CAPACITY LIVES. A separate lane is asking
// whether serving capacity belongs in gunbc.spark plus the harness at all, or in the fabric control
// plane. This makes that question EASIER: the route facts a control plane would own -- endpoint,
// front door, declared turn ceiling -- are now one small module with one shape per route, rather than
// being implicit in a convergence roster that also decides deployment. Moving them is a re-homing of
// this file, not an extraction from three.

// THE FRONT DOOR IS ASKED OF THE ROUTE THAT OWNS IT, AND NEITHER ROUTE'S PORT IS SPELLED HERE.
// A row named harness_engine_port stood in gunbc.harness.harness_wire, which is not a fact about the
// harness: it is the port a route's vLLM front door answers on, and the harness is one client of it.
// Deleting it was right; re-minting the literal here was not, and this module did that for one turn
// of review. Group A's port is the one the repository LAUNCHES that route on -- gunbc.spark.pair_serving_realization
// spark_pair_serve_port, itself derived from the observed launch -- so a hand-written 30000 beside it
// meant the port we launch on and the port we ask on were two rows free to drift. Group B was already
// right, consuming the canary arm's own port rather than spelling 30001; group A now matches it.
//
// AND THE FIELD IS A Port RATHER THAN A Nat, because std.types already carries
// `type Port = Int where range(min: 1, max: 65535)` and a module whose stated purpose is to own route
// front doors is the wrong place to decline the construction that makes an out-of-range port
// unwritable (DESIGN section 4b: construction over validation).
type ServingRouteEndpoint {
  group: FabricGroup
  address: NonEmptyStr
  port: Port
}

// ── HOW MANY TURNS A ROUTE WILL TAKE, AND WHY THAT IS A DECISION RATHER THAN A READING ───────
//
// THE NUMBER IS NOT OBSERVABLE FROM THE ROUTE, and that was established by probing rather than
// assumed: group A's live front door serves ~61KB of Prometheus text and carries no max_num_seqs and
// no scheduler-configuration series at all (manager probe, 2026-09-11). What the route DOES carry is
// its present occupancy and whether it is holding requests back for capacity, which is a different
// question and is answered elsewhere -- in the admission law, from the served gauges.
//
// SO THE CEILING IS DECLARED, TYPED AS A DECISION, AND CANNOT BE SPELLED AS A MEASUREMENT. Both arms
// below say where the number came from, and neither of them is "somebody measured this route's
// capacity", because nobody has. The attributable-allocation shape is the one
// gunbc.fleet.fleet_intent_network already uses: DESIGN section 5 forbids fabricating an
// OBSERVATION, and an allocation carrying a decider, a date and a basis is not one.
//
// WHAT THE CEILING IS FOR, WHICH IS THE HALF THAT MAKES A WRONG NUMBER CHEAP. It bounds optimism and
// nothing else. Admission takes the MINIMUM of this ceiling and what the route says about itself
// right now, so the live reading may always refuse and may never admit beyond the ceiling. A ceiling
// alone would be a static claim about a box the operator has told us to assume is unreliable and
// stateless; a live reading alone would over-admit, because an observation is stale the instant it
// is taken and a route reporting nothing running would admit every arrival at once. Under the
// minimum, a ceiling that is too high costs throughput the moment the route pushes back, and a
// ceiling that is too low costs only throughput -- neither costs correctness.
//
// AND THE STALE LAUNCH-LINE READING IS DELIBERATELY NOT CONSUMED HERE.
// gunbc.spark.pair_serving_desired spark_pair_serving_seq_capacity reads SeqCapacityMeasured with a
// count, and its own basis records that the route serves nothing and that the DeepSeek answering on
// the head's front door was launched OUT OF BAND from a container that has since exited. That row
// governs what a pair-serving LAUNCH would request; it is not a reading of the process that would
// take these turns, and inheriting its number here would have made a disowned measurement the
// authority for admission.
type ServingRouteTurnCeiling
  = CeilingByDeclaredPolicy { count: Nat, decider: NonEmptyStr, decided_on: NonEmptyStr, basis: NonEmptyStr }
  | CeilingFromRouteLaunch { count: Nat, basis: NonEmptyStr }

type ServingRoute
  = ServingRouteResolved { endpoint: ServingRouteEndpoint, ceiling: ServingRouteTurnCeiling }
  | ServingRouteUnresolved { group: FabricGroup, obligation: NonEmptyStr }

fn serving_route_turn_ceiling_count(c: ServingRouteTurnCeiling) -> Nat {
  match c {
    CeilingByDeclaredPolicy { count: n, decider: _, decided_on: _, basis: _ } => n
    CeilingFromRouteLaunch { count: n, basis: _ } => n
  }
}

// GROUP A'S CEILING IS A DECLARED ALLOCATION AND ITS DECIDER IS NAMED. Four, because it is small
// enough to be safe on a route whose real capacity nobody has measured, large enough to prove the
// admission law delivers concurrency at all, and cheap to change: superseding it is one row and no
// law. It is reported to the operator as the manager's decision rather than theirs, so an operator
// ruling replaces this row rather than arguing with it.
data serving_pair_route_turn_ceiling: ServingRouteTurnCeiling = CeilingByDeclaredPolicy {
  count: 4,
  decider: "bright-boar-435 (roadmap manager)" as NonEmptyStr,
  decided_on: "2026-09-11" as NonEmptyStr,
  basis: "nothing derives it and nothing on the route reports it: the live front door carries no max_num_seqs and no scheduler-configuration series, probed 2026-09-11. It is an attributable allocation bounding how optimistic admission may be, always taken as the MINIMUM with the route's own present-tense capacity signal, so a wrong value costs throughput and never correctness. It is NOT a capacity measurement and must never be re-spelled as one" as NonEmptyStr,
}

// EACH ROUTE'S CEILING COMES FROM ITS OWN AUTHORITY, which is why this is a match and not a table.
// Group B's is the canary arm's own declared bound, because the canary arm IS the engine answering
// on that route -- gunbc.spark.glm_canary_converge is its convergence root -- so its bound is a
// statement about the process that would take the turns rather than about a launch nobody performed.
// The dispatch that selects between two route authorities is realization and belongs at this
// periphery (DESIGN section 3), never inside either of them.
fn serving_route_ceiling(group: FabricGroup) -> ServingRouteTurnCeiling {
  match group {
    FabricGroupA => serving_pair_route_turn_ceiling
    FabricGroupB => serving_canary_route_ceiling(bound: glm_canary_arm.max_num_seqs)
  }
}

// THE CANARY'S TWO ARMS STAY TWO ARMS. An operator's experimental bound and a reading off the launch
// line that is running are different claims about where a number came from, and flattening them here
// would lose exactly the provenance this type exists to carry. Each basis travels forward unedited.
fn serving_canary_route_ceiling(bound: SeqBound) -> ServingRouteTurnCeiling {
  match bound {
    ExperimentalOperatorBound { count: n, basis: b } =>
      CeilingByDeclaredPolicy { count: n, decider: "operator" as NonEmptyStr, decided_on: "2026-09-08" as NonEmptyStr, basis: b }
    MeasuredFromLaunchLine { count: n, basis: b } => CeilingFromRouteLaunch { count: n, basis: b }
  }
}

fn serving_route_head(group: FabricGroup) -> HostIdentity {
  match group {
    FabricGroupA => spark_pair_serving_head(group: FabricGroupA)
    FabricGroupB => glm_canary_arm.host
  }
}

// THE CANARY'S PORT CROSSES ONE WIDTH BOUNDARY AND IT IS CROSSED HERE, ONCE. Its arm declares the
// port as a Nat; Port is a bounded Int. The conversion is done at this seam rather than at each
// consumer so there is exactly one place where the canary's spelling meets the corpus's port type,
// and the range refinement is what checks the result.
fn serving_route_port(group: FabricGroup) -> Port {
  match group {
    FabricGroupA => spark_pair_serve_port
    FabricGroupB => glm_canary_arm.port as Port
  }
}

// A HEAD WITH NO LAN ENDPOINT ROW IS UNRESOLVED, NEVER A SHORTER LIST. The address is the fleet
// topology's, not this module's: a route whose head has no endpoint row is a route we cannot ask,
// and the refusal names the group so the count of what is unreachable is observable rather than
// absorbed into a roster that quietly got smaller.
fn serving_route(group: FabricGroup) -> ServingRoute {
  match host_lan_address(host: serving_route_head(group: group)) {
    Absent =>
      ServingRouteUnresolved {
        group: group,
        obligation: join([fabric_group_wire(g: group) as String, ": the route's head has no LAN endpoint row in gunbc.fleet_intent_network, so there is no address to ask"], "") as NonEmptyStr,
      }
    Present { value: address } =>
      ServingRouteResolved {
        endpoint: ServingRouteEndpoint { group: group, address: address, port: serving_route_port(group: group) },
        ceiling: serving_route_ceiling(group: group),
      }
  }
}

// ── ON KEYING A ROUTE BY A FABRIC GROUP, WHICH IS A CAGE ─────────────────────────────────────
//
// THIS MODULE DOES NOT WIDEN FabricGroup AND DELIBERATELY DOES NOT FIX IT EITHER, so here is which
// of the two it did. FabricGroup is a closed two-variant enum keyed by PHYSICAL CAGE -- the legs of
// one 400G switch cage -- and the corpus already uses that one discriminant for three concepts: a
// network group, a serving identity, and a seat-pool partition key. A cage is not a serving
// identity: one cage could serve two models, and one model could be served by two cages.
//
// WHAT THIS CHANGE DOES TO THAT. It moves the route's real facts OFF the cage and onto the route --
// the endpoint, the front door, the declared ceiling, and (through the probe) the model actually
// being served are properties of a ServingRoute, not of a FabricGroup -- so the cage survives here
// as the route's NAME and nothing more. The offer identity downstream was already route-shaped
// rather than cage-shaped: gunbc.spark.vllm_endpoint_process_launch keys an offer on the endpoint
// and the answering process, so a restart is already a different subject.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. The seat partition is still keyed by cage plus class, so two models served
// from one cage would share one tolerant pool -- which is the first thing that breaks if this fleet
// grows the second-model-per-cage case the operator's model-agnostic ruling makes plausible. That is
// named here as the growth surface rather than pre-solved: the trigger is a route identity of its
// own, at which point the partition keys on the route and the cage becomes an attribute of it. A
// route identity minted now, with two routes that happen to sit one per cage, would be a second name
// for a distinction nothing yet exercises.
//
// A NOTE ON THE WORD FABRIC, because this module sits between both senses: FabricGroup here is the
// NETWORK interconnect sense (gunbc.spark.fabric_switch_observed), while the selection and capacity
// machinery this feeds is the COMPUTE fabric sense (product.fabric.selection). They are two concepts
// under one word and that fork is not this module's to resolve; every use above is the network one.

// BOTH GROUPS ARE ENROLLED. This row is the whole of what "enrolled" means: these are the routes a
// turn may be offered to, subject to every one of them answering a probe first. It says nothing
// about whose hosts are claimed, which engine is desired, or what is converged -- those stay with
// gunbc.spark.pair_serving_desired and gunbc.spark.glm_canary_converge respectively, and neither is
// widened by this row.
data serving_enrolled_groups: List<FabricGroup> = [FabricGroupA, FabricGroupB]

fn serving_enrolled_routes() -> List<ServingRoute> {
  map(serving_enrolled_groups, g => serving_route(group: g))
}

fn serving_route_endpoint_wire(e: ServingRouteEndpoint) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([e.address as String, ":", to_string(e.port)], "") as NonEmptyStr
}
